  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "The graph of $y = \\sin(x - 30^\\circ)$ is the graph of $y = \\sin(x)$ slid sideways. How many degrees does it slide to the RIGHT?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "30",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "A phase shift slides the whole wave sideways. Look at the number subtracted inside the brackets.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "A phase shift slides the whole wave sideways. Look at the number subtracted inside the brackets."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "In $y = \\sin(x - c)$, the wave slides $c$ degrees to the RIGHT. Here $c = 30$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "It slides $30^\\circ$ to the right."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Plain $\\sin(x)$ starts its climb at $x = 0$.",
    "For $\\sin(x - 30^\\circ)$, the inside is zero when $x = 30^\\circ$, so the wave starts its climb $30^\\circ$ later.",
    "Starting later means the whole wave has slid $30^\\circ$ to the RIGHT."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Ask when the inside is zero",
      "steps": [
        "Whatever plain sine did at 0, the shifted wave does when the inside equals 0.",
        "x − 30 = 0 gives x = 30. Every feature arrives 30° later, which is a slide right."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The late-running tide",
      "steps": [
        "Think of high tide arriving 30 minutes later than usual each day.",
        "The tide pattern is exactly the same shape, just running late. Running late = sliding right on a time graph."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "phase-shift"
  ],
